# T-2558 (T-2468 antifragility lens) — Dead-letter canary (G-063 class, durable poison-drop sink)
# Source of truth: /opt/termlink/.context/cron/dead-letter-canary.crontab (git-tracked)
# Installed to:    /etc/cron.d/termlink-dead-letter-canary (USER field syntax — do NOT load into a user crontab)
#
# T-2243 (R4) stopped SILENT poison-drops on the "exchange durable messages" path:
# a `channel post` rejected POISON_THRESHOLD=10 times is now durably MOVED to the
# offline queue's dead_letters table instead of DELETE'd. But that record is
# surfaced only if a human runs `/queue-status` — nothing reads it on a schedule
# (the T-2083–2087 queue observability arc fires only on Drained/Pending, never on
# dead-letter growth). So a poison-dropped GUARANTEED message sits forever with
# nothing firing — the "write-only sink nobody noticed" class (G-063). This daily
# canary reads `channel queue-status --json` and FIRES (exit 1) when dead_letters>0.
# Empty log = healthy. /canaries auto-discovers this log.
#
# Operator action on firing: a guaranteed post was rejected 10x and dead-lettered.
# Inspect with `/queue-status` (full rows), fix the reject cause (unknown topic →
# create it; bad signature → `fleet reauth`), re-send via `/agent-handoff`, or drop
# the row from ~/.termlink/outbound.sqlite if dead. Ad-hoc: bash scripts/check-dead-letter-freshness.sh
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin

# Daily at 07:39 UTC — offset from the :27 stuck-claims, :33 session-control,
# :17 unconfirmed-delivery, :13 mirror canaries.
39 7 * * * root cd /opt/termlink && bash scripts/check-dead-letter-freshness.sh --quiet >> .context/working/.dead-letter-canary.log 2>> .context/working/.dead-letter-canary.log.stderr
